## The Bug

The condition `decoded.exp < Date.now() / 1000` uses strict less-than, so when `exp === now` the check passes and the expired token is accepted. The fix is `<=`.

However, there's a deeper issue: **`jwt.verify` already validates expiry** and throws `TokenExpiredError` when `exp <= now`. The manual check is both wrong and redundant — and the catch block swallows all errors identically, hiding useful distinctions.

The correct approach is to remove the manual check entirely and handle `jsonwebtoken`'s typed errors properly.

```javascript
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function authMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
  }

  try {
    // jwt.verify throws TokenExpiredError when exp <= now (inclusive), so no
    // manual expiry check is needed — and avoids the off-by-one bug.
    req.user = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    next();
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'TokenExpiredError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
    }
    if (err.name === 'JsonWebTokenError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
    }
    // Unexpected error (e.g. bad secret config) — don't leak internals
    next(err);
  }
}

module.exports = authMiddleware;
```

**What changed and why:**

| Issue | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Off-by-one on expiry | `exp < now` accepts `exp === now` | Removed; `jwt.verify` uses `<=` correctly |
| Error specificity | All errors → `"Invalid token"` | `TokenExpiredError` vs `JsonWebTokenError` vs unexpected |
| Unexpected errors | Silently swallowed | Forwarded to Express error handler via `next(err)` |
